INT. HOTEL BALLROOM - AFTER THE WEDDING - NIGHT

Folding chairs askew. A deflated balloon arch. Half-eaten cake under a plastic dome. Streamers on the floor like cut ribbon. The chandelier still burns, as if nobody has told it.

Nothing moves. A clock ticks.

A side door opens. Heels on parquet.

A WOMAN in a green evening dress, 50s, steps in, shoes in hand. Mascara tracked down one cheek. She surveys the wreckage and her mouth tightens.

She crosses to the head table. Finds a tape recorder among the centerpieces, the kind guests were meant to speak into. She lifts it. Examines it. Presses record.

WOMAN
Testing. One, two.

She stops. Rewinds. Plays back.

WOMAN (V.O.)
(recorded, faint)
Testing. One, two.

She nods. Records again.

WOMAN (CONT'D)
This is Catalina Rousseau-Mbeki, mother of the groom. I would like to say something I didn't say at the toast.

She stops. Her jaw works. She sits on the edge of the stage.

CATALINA
I'm sorry I wore green. I was told it wasn't hers.

She lowers the recorder to her lap. Looks at the cake. Rewinds. Plays the last of it.

CATALINA (V.O.)
(recorded)
I'm sorry I wore green.

A phone rings in her purse. She lets it ring. It rings again.

CATALINA
(to the ceiling)
Not tonight.

The phone stops. She raises the recorder.

CATALINA (CONT'D)
(to the tape)
He looked happy. That's all. Remember that. He looked happy.

She sets the recorder on the stage, still running. Picks up her shoes. Crosses to the exit. At the door, she turns off the chandelier switch.

Darkness. A tiny red light glows on the stage.
